<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
	<modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
	<parent>
		<groupId>com.ecomdemo</groupId>
		<artifactId>ecomdemo-parent</artifactId>
		<version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
	</parent>
	<artifactId>ecomdemo-app</artifactId>
	<name>ecomdemo-app</name>
	<description>The application, still one deployable. The services are carved out of this.</description>

	<!--
		Still the whole application: every module except the three that became `common`. Phase 20
		extracts the services from here one at a time, and this module is what is left over - it
		becomes order-service once catalog, inventory, customer and notification have gone.

		The dependency list is the original one, unchanged. Narrowing it is the extractions' job:
		a service that does not consume from Kafka should not carry the Kafka starter, and the
		honest moment to decide that is when the service exists.
	-->
	<dependencies>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>com.ecomdemo</groupId>
			<artifactId>ecomdemo-common</artifactId>
			<version>${project.version}</version>
		</dependency>

		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			springdoc-openapi scans the controllers at startup, builds an OpenAPI 3 document from
			the mappings, the DTO records and the Bean Validation constraints, serves it at
			/v3/api-docs and bundles Swagger UI to render it. 3.1.1 is the first GA line built
			against Spring Boot 4 / Spring Framework 7; the 2.x line targets Boot 3.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
			<artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
			<version>${springdoc.version}</version>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Spring Security. Adding this one starter changes the application before a line of
			our code runs: it registers a servlet Filter that sits in front of every request,
			and its auto-configuration locks the whole application down by default. That default
			is the point — a new endpoint added in a later phase is protected until somebody
			deliberately opens it, rather than public until somebody remembers to close it.
			`SecurityConfig` replaces the defaults with this application's own rules.

			It also brings `spring-security-crypto`, which is where BCryptPasswordEncoder lives,
			so password hashing needs no separate dependency. The version comes from the Spring
			Boot BOM (Spring Security 7.x for Boot 4.x).
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Spring Cache plus Redis.

			`spring-boot-starter-cache` is the abstraction: @Cacheable and friends, and the
			proxying that makes them work. It does nothing on its own - with no cache provider on
			the classpath Spring falls back to a ConcurrentHashMap, which is a cache per JVM and
			therefore no cache at all once there are two instances.

			`spring-boot-starter-data-redis` supplies the provider. It brings Lettuce, the default
			Redis client since Boot 2 (netty-based, non-blocking, and one connection is safe to
			share across threads - Jedis needed a pool). Versions come from the Spring Boot BOM.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-cache</artifactId>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-redis</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Spring Batch. One starter brings the whole framework: Job, Step, the chunk-oriented
			reader/processor/writer contract, the fault-tolerant skip and retry machinery, and -
			the part that makes it more than a for-loop - the JobRepository, which records every
			run in the database so that a failed job can be restarted from where it stopped
			rather than from the beginning.

			`spring-boot-starter-batch` pulls `spring-boot-batch`, which is Boot 4's
			auto-configuration module for it (Boot 4 splits auto-configuration into a module per
			technology, so `spring-batch-core` on its own would wire up nothing). That
			auto-configuration needs a DataSource and a transaction manager, both of which this
			application already has from spring-boot-starter-data-jpa.

			Versions come from the Spring Boot BOM (Spring Batch 6.x for Boot 4.x).
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-batch</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Actuator (Phase 15). It adds the /actuator endpoints - health, info, metrics - and,
			more importantly, the Micrometer MeterRegistry that everything else in this phase
			builds on. Spring Boot auto-instruments what it already owns the moment the starter
			is present: HTTP request timings, the HikariCP pool, the JVM's heap, GC and threads,
			Hibernate's statistics, the Redis cache from Phase 13 and the Spring Batch jobs from
			Phase 14 all start publishing meters without a line of code from us.

			Nothing is exposed over HTTP by default except /actuator/health: the endpoints a
			deployment actually publishes are an explicit list in application.properties.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			The Prometheus registry. Micrometer is a facade - meters are recorded against its API
			and a registry decides what that means for one monitoring system. This one holds the
			values in memory and renders them, on request, in Prometheus' text exposition format
			at /actuator/prometheus. That is the whole of the "pull model": the application never
			connects to Prometheus, it just answers when Prometheus asks.

			Swapping this artifact for micrometer-registry-otlp or -datadog would change the
			destination without touching a single instrumented line, which is the point of the
			facade. The version comes from the Spring Boot BOM.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
			<artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
			<scope>runtime</scope>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Spring Security's OAuth2 Resource Server. "Resource server" is the OAuth2 word for
			an API that ACCEPTS tokens rather than one that issues them: it adds a filter that
			pulls a Bearer token off the Authorization header, verifies its signature and its
			expiry, and turns the claims into an Authentication. All of that used to be the job
			of BasicAuthenticationFilter plus a database lookup on every request.

			Strictly, this application is both halves — it also ISSUES the token, at
			/api/auth/login — which a textbook OAuth2 deployment would split into a separate
			authorization server. That is the honest simplification of this phase, and the README
			says so. The starter brings `spring-security-oauth2-jose` with it, which carries
			Nimbus JOSE and therefore both the decoder AND the encoder, so signing needs no
			further dependency (and in particular no third-party JWT library).

			The version comes from the Spring Boot BOM (Spring Security 7.1.1).
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			The PostgreSQL JDBC driver. It is only ever loaded by reflection through the JDBC URL,
			so runtime scope is enough - no application code imports it. The version comes from
			the Spring Boot BOM, which pins a driver known to work with the managed HikariCP.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
			<artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
			<scope>runtime</scope>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Flyway. On startup it scans classpath:db/migration, works out from its own
			`flyway_schema_history` table which versioned scripts this database has not run yet,
			and applies them in order, each inside a transaction.

			Two artifacts, for two different reasons:

			* `spring-boot-starter-flyway` brings `flyway-core` AND Spring Boot's Flyway
			  auto-configuration. Spring Boot 4 split auto-configuration out of one fat jar into
			  a module per technology, so - unlike Boot 3 - putting `flyway-core` on the
			  classpath by itself wires up nothing at all: the migrations are simply never run
			  and the application starts against whatever schema it happens to find.
			* Since Flyway 10, each database engine lives in its own module, so
			  `flyway-database-postgresql` carries the PostgreSQL dialect. (H2, which the test
			  suite uses, is one of the two still bundled inside flyway-core.)

			Both versions come from the Spring Boot BOM, so they match each other and the driver.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-flyway</artifactId>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
			<artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			H2, on the test classpath only. Phase 7 was pencilled in as the phase that would
			delete it; it stays, and that is a deliberate choice rather than an oversight.

			The two suites now answer two different questions. The unit and slice tests
			(Surefire, `test` profile, H2) ask "is the Java right?" - they run in seconds, on any
			machine, with no Docker, and they are what a developer runs constantly. The
			integration tests (Failsafe, `it` profile, Testcontainers) ask "is this true of
			PostgreSQL?" - they cost a container start and they are where an H2-only truth would
			be caught. Deleting H2 would buy honesty in the fast suite at the price of making it
			slow, which is the trade the testing pyramid exists to avoid.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
			<artifactId>h2</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa-test</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation-test</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc-test</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			`spring-security-test`, by way of Boot 4's starter. It contributes @WithMockUser and
			the SecurityMockMvcRequestPostProcessors that let a slice test say "run this request
			as an ADMIN" without a real login, and it is what makes the 401/403 assertions in the
			controller tests possible at all — a @WebMvcTest builds the security filter chain,
			so without an authenticated user every request in those tests would simply be a 401.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security-test</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Apache Kafka (Phase 17). `spring-kafka` brings the `kafka-clients` library plus
			Spring's wrapper over it: `KafkaTemplate` for producing, `@KafkaListener` and the
			listener container for consuming, and the `@RetryableTopic` machinery that creates
			the retry and dead-letter topics.

			No version: Spring Boot manages both. Boot 4.1.1 pins spring-kafka 4.1.1 and
			kafka-clients 4.2.1 - and compose runs the broker on `apache/kafka:4.2.1`, the same
			number on purpose. The Kafka protocol is versioned and negotiated, so a mismatch
			usually works; matching them means the client never silently falls back to an older
			protocol version and behaves differently here than it would against the broker it
			was tested with.

			THE STARTER, NOT `org.springframework.kafka:spring-kafka`. Boot 4 split its
			auto-configuration out of the monolithic `spring-boot-autoconfigure` into a module per
			technology, so the library on its own now gives you the classes and none of the
			wiring: `KafkaAutoConfiguration` lives in `spring-boot-kafka`, which this starter
			brings. The symptom of getting it wrong is not a missing class but a missing BEAN -
			"required a bean of type 'KafkaTemplate' that could not be found" - which reads like
			an application mistake rather than a dependency one.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-kafka</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Testcontainers. `testcontainers-postgresql` starts a real PostgreSQL server in a
			Docker container for the duration of the tests and throws it away afterwards, so the
			integration tests run against the same engine production uses instead of an H2
			imitation of it. In Testcontainers 2.x every database module is named
			`testcontainers-<engine>`; the 1.x coordinates were `org.testcontainers:postgresql`.

			`spring-boot-testcontainers` is the bridge: it is what makes @ServiceConnection work,
			so a container declared as a @Bean supplies the JDBC url, username and password to
			the application context by itself. Without it the container would start and the test
			would still connect to whatever spring.datasource.url happened to say.

			There is deliberately no `testcontainers-junit-jupiter`: its @Testcontainers/@Container
			extension is the alternative to letting Spring own the container lifecycle, and this
			phase lets Spring own it (see PostgresContainerConfig).

			Versions come from the Spring Boot BOM (testcontainers 2.0.5).
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-testcontainers</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
			<artifactId>testcontainers-kafka</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
			<artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			`TestRestTemplate` - the client the integration tests use to call the running
			application - is a thin wrapper over `RestTemplate`, and it builds one through
			`RestTemplateBuilder`. Spring Boot 4 moved that builder into its own
			`spring-boot-restclient` module, and this application never makes an outbound HTTP
			call, so nothing else drags it in: without this line the context fails to start with
			`ClassNotFoundException: org.springframework.boot.restclient.RestTemplateBuilder`.
			Test scope, because it is the test client that needs it and not the application.
		-->
		<!--
			COMPILE scope since Phase 20b, and the change is the split becoming visible in the
			build. The comment above described this as a TEST dependency because "this application
			never makes an outbound HTTP call" - which stopped being true the moment inventory
			became a service. InventoryClient needs the auto-configured RestClient.Builder at
			runtime, not just in tests.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-restclient</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Spring Modulith, TEST SCOPE ONLY, and both halves of that are deliberate.

			Scope first. This phase wants three things: named modules, a test that fails when one
			reaches into another's internals, and generated diagrams. All three are build-time
			concerns - `ApplicationModules` reads the package structure with ArchUnit and
			`Documenter` writes PlantUML from it. None of them run in production, so none of them
			belong on the runtime classpath. `spring-modulith-starter-core` would also pull the
			runtime auto-configuration and the event publication registry, which this phase does
			not use: Phase 18 already built a durable outbox, and a second event-persistence
			mechanism alongside it would be two answers to one question.

			Version second, and this is the sharp edge. Spring Boot 4.1.1 does NOT manage Spring
			Modulith - it is absent from `spring-boot-dependencies` - and the newest GA line,
			1.4.x, targets Boot 3.5 and Spring Framework 6.2. Keeping it test-scoped is what makes
			that mismatch tolerable: the application context is never assembled from these jars,
			so the Boot-4 auto-configuration changes that would break the starter cannot be
			reached. `spring-modulith-core` needs little more than ArchUnit and Spring's core
			type-scanning, and `ModularityTest` is the proof that it holds on this stack.
		-->
		<!--
			The annotations only - `@ApplicationModule` and `@NamedInterface` - and these DO go on
			main source, because a module's boundary is declared by the code that owns it and not
			by the test that checks it. COMPILE scope for that reason, unlike the two below.

			It is an annotations jar in practice: its three dependencies (spring-boot-autoconfigure,
			spring-tx, spring-context) are all declared OPTIONAL, so none of them is pulled in
			transitively and none of Boot's auto-configuration comes with it. That is what makes it
			safe to put on the runtime classpath of an application whose Boot version this Modulith
			line does not officially target.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-modulith-api</artifactId>
			<version>${spring-modulith.version}</version>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-modulith-core</artifactId>
			<version>${spring-modulith.version}</version>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-modulith-docs</artifactId>
			<version>${spring-modulith.version}</version>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
	</dependencies>

	<!--
		The plugins this module opts into. Every one of them is CONFIGURED in the parent's
		<pluginManagement>; naming it here is what makes it run, with nothing repeated.

		spring-boot-maven-plugin appears here and not in `common` on purpose: it repackages a jar
		into an executable application, which is right for a deployable and a build failure for a
		library with no main class.
	-->
	<build>
		<plugins>
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
				<artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
			</plugin>
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
			</plugin>
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
			</plugin>
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
			</plugin>
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
				<artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
			</plugin>
		</plugins>
	</build>
</project>
